\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We measure the generational depth discrimination boundary $\Kempirical$ for recursive synthetic text: web text yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (8/9 configurations, 5 families, 1B--14B; $p \leq 0.002$), academic text $\Kempirical{=}3$ (5 models, 4 families).
Largely generator-independent features enable 80--92\% accuracy retention ($\rho > 0.90$); $\Kempirical{=}2$ supports binary filtering as the optimal granularity for web text.
All main results use prompt-chain (retrain-chain $\Kempirical{\geq}4$; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}); the gap to $\Ktheory$ may narrow with richer features, scaling beyond 14B, and retrain-chain characterization.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_kstar}
\label{sec:one_wash}
\label{sec:negative_graduated}
\label{sec:repr_dependence}
\label{sec:boundary_cases}
\label{sec:length_confound}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_jsd_decay.pdf}
\caption{Token-level JSD between adjacent depth pairs on log scale. Blue band: min-max range across 8 C4 models (1B--14B) with $\Kempirical{=}2$; dashed purple: Mistral-7B ($\Kempirical{=}1$, cross-scorer validated); green: arXiv Pythia ($\Kempirical{=}3$); dotted red: retrain-chain ($\Kempirical{\geq}4$). C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$), while arXiv decays gradually ($\lambda \approx 1.0$) and retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$).}
\label{fig:jsd_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{One-wash laundering (web text).}
For general web text, $\Kempirical{=}2$ reflects concentrated information loss: 0v1 is near-trivially separable ($\auc \geq 0.92$), 1v2 drops by ${\sim}$30\,pp, and 2v3 falls below $\theta$.
A single generation pass renders text indistinguishable from multiply recycled content, limiting depth-aware curation to a binary boundary.
Converging evidence supports a data-intrinsic interpretation: OBD achieves 96\% depth-0 recall but only 14--22\% for depths 2--4; frontier LLMs show 8--38\% (McNemar $p > 0.05$ vs.\ OBD; Table~\ref{tab:s3_llm_zeroshot}).
Baseline classifier substitution (logistic regression, linear SVC, random forest) yields non-significant McNemar differences in 31/36 comparisons (Table~\ref{tab:mcnemar_obd_baselines}).

\paragraph{JSD decay and the discrimination boundary.}
Token-level Jensen-Shannon Divergence (JSD) between adjacent generations reveals two distinct decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}): C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff (0v1 JSD exceeds 1v2 by 6--56$\times$, median ${\sim}$15$\times$) followed by rapid convergence to a noise floor, while arXiv decays ${\sim}$3$\times$ slower ($\lambda \approx 1.0$ vs.\ $2.9$).
Cross-model consistency indicates that $G_\delta$ contraction rate is domain-governed.
The retrain-chain profile ($\lambda = 0.285$, no cliff) reflects model-adaptation artifacts rather than text-intrinsic loss: retrain-chain $\Kempirical{\geq}4$ (Pythia-1.4B LoRA; Table~\ref{tab:retrain_ci}) far exceeds prompt-chain $\Kempirical{=}2$; since all main results use prompt-chain, the reported boundaries are conservative lower bounds (Appendix~\ref{sec:jsd_extended}).

\paragraph{Graduated filtering failure.}
For web text, 2v3 $\auc < \theta$ across all configurations, so depth assignments beyond $d{=}2$ approximate random labels and graduated weighting injects noise (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).
The strategy ordering binary $>$ graduated aligns with $\Kempirical{=}2$; on arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$), graduated filtering similarly shows no improvement, consistent with $\Kempirical$ capturing discriminability rather than curation utility.

\paragraph{Representation and scorer dependence.}
$\Kempirical$ is representation-dependent: frozen Qwen embeddings yield $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$, while Pythia and OLMo match the 15D boundary at $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$ (Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}); the 15D features trade one depth level for cross-model consistency (8/9 configurations $\Kempirical{\leq}2$).
$\Kempirical$ can also depend on scorer family: on Wikipedia, cross-family scoring reduces $\Kempirical$ from 2 to 1 ($\Delta = -2.4$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}), while on C4 it preserves feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$) and $\Kempirical$ for 6/8 configurations (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}); scorer sensitivity is inversely related to domain lexical diversity.

\paragraph{Domain dependence of $\Kempirical$.}
C4 web text yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (8/9 configurations, 1B--14B), arXiv yields $\Kempirical{=}3$ (five models, four families; Section~\ref{sec:cross_domain}), and Wikipedia yields $\Kempirical{=}1$ with scorer confound controlled (Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}), producing a consistent domain ordering that correlates with lexical diversity (Table~\ref{tab:domain-ttr-entropy}).
The only C4 exception (Mistral-7B, $\Kempirical{=}1$) has $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv, reinforcing the domain-governed interpretation (Appendix~\ref{sec:boundary_extended}); a LaTeX markup ablation confirms content-level properties dominate (2v3 $\Delta = -1.8$\,pp, $\Kempirical$ unchanged; Appendix~\ref{sec:latex_ablation}).

\paragraph{Why $\Kempirical$ matters.}
$\Kempirical{=}2$ for web text is a precise measurement that rules out both $\Kempirical{=}1$ (no depth signal beyond 0v1) and $\Kempirical{\geq}3$ (graduated tracking feasible), while arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ confirms the framework captures real domain-level variation.
For curation, $\Kempirical{=}2$ validates binary filtering for web text (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}) and provides post-hoc grounding for the binary approach of \citet{drayson2025}.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/pipeline.pdf}
\caption{Generational depth estimation pipeline. Seed documents are recursively passed through a generation operator $G_\delta$ to produce a depth chain. Statistical features are fed to the OBD-LightGBM classifier for depth prediction.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}

A generation operator $G_\delta$ (LLM + decoding strategy $\delta$) produces successive generations $X \to Y_1 \to \cdots \to Y_K$, where $Y_k = G_\delta(Y_{k-1})$ and $Y_0 \triangleq X$ (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).
This sequence forms a Markov chain.
By the Data Processing Inequality, $I(X; Y_1) \geq I(X; Y_2) \geq \cdots \geq 0$: the original signal degrades monotonically.
Together with Markov chain convergence, this motivates (under mild regularity conditions, e.g., bounded per-step generation divergence) a finite $\Ktheory$ beyond which adjacent generations become indistinguishable.

We define the operational lower bound:
\begin{equation}
  \Kempirical = \max\{k \geq 1 : \auc_{k-1,k} > \theta\},
  \label{eq:kstar}
\end{equation}
where $\auc_{k-1,k}$ is the point-estimate pairwise AUC (Area Under the ROC Curve) and $\theta = 0.60$ is a post-hoc operational threshold (motivated below).
Bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals (CIs; 1{,}000 resamples\footnote{Boundary-pair results in Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci} use 1{,}000 resamples; arXiv cross-scorer CIs (Table~\ref{tab:arxiv_crossscorer_ci}) use 10{,}000 for tighter bounds at the margin.}) provide uncertainty quantification: we verify that boundary-pair CI lower bounds exceed $\theta$ and non-boundary-pair CI upper bounds fall below $\theta$ (Section~\ref{sec:kstar_boundary}).

\paragraph{Threshold choice.}
We set $\theta{=}0.60$ as a post-hoc operational threshold, chosen at the empirical discontinuity in the AUC distribution (Figure~\ref{fig:kstar_theta}).
A 10{,}000-iteration permutation test yields a 95th-percentile null of ${<}\,0.55$ and $p < 0.002$ for all boundary comparisons, placing $\theta{=}0.60$ well above the empirical null.
$\Kempirical{=}2$ is stable across $\theta \in [0.55, 0.65]$ for all but two marginal configurations (Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci_extended}): 1v2 AUC values cluster at $0.61$--$0.70$ while 2v3 values cluster at $0.55$--$0.59$, creating a ${\sim}$5--11\,pp discontinuity.
Only at extremes does $\Kempirical$ shift: $\theta{=}0.55$ yields $\Kempirical{=}3$ for two Qwen configurations; $\theta{=}0.70$ yields $\Kempirical{=}1$ across all configurations.
The downstream filtering evaluation (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}) uses the OBD classifier without re-optimizing $\theta$, avoiding circularity.
A leave-one-family-out (LOFO) cross-validation on C4 independently validates this choice: each of five folds derives $\hat{\theta}$ from the gap midpoint between $\min(\auc_{1v2})$ and $\max(\auc_{2v3})$ over the remaining families, yielding $\hat{\theta} \in [0.5725, 0.6004]$; $\theta{=}0.60$ falls within this range, and 8/9 held-out predictions match the original $\Kempirical$ (the single mismatch is the borderline rewrite-mode configuration; Table~\ref{tab:theta_lofo}).
ArXiv corroborates with 6/6 LOFO agreement; Wikipedia is underdetermined because most models produce near-chance 1v2 AUC, invalidating the gap-based derivation (Appendix~\ref{sec:theta_lofo}).


\subsection{Features and Classifier}
\label{sec:features}

Each document is characterized by a 15-dimensional feature vector spanning four groups: \emph{surprisal moments} (mean, std, skewness, kurtosis; 4~dims), \emph{surprisal derivatives} (mean and std of first- and second-order differences, plus first-order skewness; 5~dims), \emph{vocabulary diversity} (type-token ratio, hapax legomena ratio, self-BLEU; 3~dims), and \emph{frequency tail statistics} (frequency kurtosis, entropy, rare token fraction; 3~dims).
All features are computed from token-level surprisal values via an external scorer model (full definitions in Table~\ref{tab:s5_features}, Appendix).
Self-BLEU is computed within each CV fold using only same-fold, same-depth reference samples to prevent leakage; it ranks \#1 in 1/8 conditions (greedy decoding) but is absent from the top-3 in all 7 others.
Removing self-BLEU reduces mean $\auc_{1v2}$ by $< 1$\,pp across all models (14D $\Kempirical = 2$ for all 4 configurations; bootstrap CIs in Table~\ref{tab:selfbleu_ablation}); in deployment where depth labels are unavailable, self-BLEU can be replaced with corpus-level self-similarity or omitted with minimal performance loss.
Hand-crafted features enable cross-generator transfer without retraining and permit attribution of discriminative power to specific text properties.

We use \emph{Ordinal Binary Decomposition} (OBD), decomposing the $(K{+}1)$-class problem into $K$ cumulative binary sub-classifiers, each estimating $P(\text{depth} > k \mid \mathbf{x})$ via LightGBM~\cite{ke2017lightgbm} (hyperparameters in Table~\ref{tab:s6_lgbm_params}).
OBD achieves lower MAE than multiclass LightGBM (e.g., 0.984 vs.\ 1.016 on Qwen-rewrite) while maintaining superior pairwise $\auc$ (mean 0.6095 vs.\ 0.4883 for ordinal logistic regression).

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We evaluate nine configurations spanning five architecture families (Table~\ref{tab:setup}): Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (rewrite and continuation modes), Qwen2.5-1.5B (base, continuation), Qwen2.5-7B, Qwen2.5-14B, Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, LLaMA-3.1-8B, and Mistral-7B (all continuation except Qwen-rw).
Each uses 5{,}000 C4 seeds truncated to 256 tokens, recursively generated for $K{=}5$ iterations (30{,}000 documents per configuration).
Default decoding: nucleus $p{=}0.95$, $T{=}1.0$; decoding ablation covers greedy, $T{=}0.7$, and $T{=}1.2$.
Continuation mode truncates seeds to 128 tokens and extends to 256; rewrite mode paraphrases the full text.
Evaluation: 5-fold GroupKFold cross-validation grouped by seed document; pairwise $\auc$ as primary metric.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Experimental configurations for depth-chain generation. \textbf{Config}: shorthand identifier; \textbf{Model}: architecture and parameter count; \textbf{Mode}: generation strategy (continuation or rewrite); $N$: number of seed documents per depth level.}
\label{tab:setup}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
